{"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"undefined-record-repository","classStatement":"The follow-up's repository is undefined for review findings whose path names no configured alias, and this is neither handled nor refused.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1: \"`repository` (the configured alias the finding's `path` starts with, or the candidate's first repository for a `requirements/` or `planning/` path)\". AC5: \"A `new-work` decision files a GitHub issue in the follow-up's repository\". Neither AC1 nor the Failure model ('Outside the model, GateReeve detects and refuses...') says what happens when a review finding's `path` starts with no configured alias and is not under requirements/ or planning/. Examples: a repository-relative path without the alias prefix such as `cli/test/x.js`, a bare `PROTOCOL.md`, or a non-path such as `rubric`. design.md only gives the example `repository/src/x.js`.","failureScenario":"Needs only ordinary reviewer output. A retained boundary review reports a non-blocking medium whose `path` is `cli/src/core.js`, but the configured alias is `gatereeve`. The derived record's `repository` is undefined. A policy-default `new-work` then either fails at issue filing, files in an arbitrary repository, or makes derivation throw so Completion reports 'follow-ups unavailable'. The behaviour depends on the implementer, and no test in R1/R5 pins it.","id":"F1","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"nonbinary-rubric-count","classStatement":"A rubric pass condition counts refusals that don't match the criterion's list, so pass and fail can't be told apart.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"R3 Pass: \"...and each of the six refusals with nothing recorded\". AC3 lists: \"An unknown ID, an empty selection, both or neither of `followUps` and `select`, both or neither of `decision` and `defaults`, or an unknown field\". That is five categories, or seven cases if each both/neither is split; neither count is six. AC3 also bounds `followUps` to \"1 to 512 IDs\" without listing over-512 as a refusal. It doesn't say whether 'empty selection' means `followUps: []`, a `select` matching no records, or `select: {}`.","failureScenario":"In ordinary implementation and gate review, the implementer tests six of the seven distinct refusal cases, for example leaving out 'neither decision nor defaults' or a 513-ID list. R3 is then judged PASS by one reader and FAIL by another. Or an untested path, such as a `select: {}` that decides every follow-up, ships as accepted behaviour.","id":"F2","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"batch-partial-failure-unspecified","classStatement":"How a multi-ID decision call behaves when one issue filing fails is left unspecified, though both readings can be recovered from.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3: \"It records one `FOLLOW_UP_DECIDED` event listing each decided ID\". AC5: \"A failed `gh` call records no decision for that ID and the operation fails naming the follow-up.\" Neither says whether the other IDs in the same call (issues already created, or low accept-risk decisions) are recorded in the one event when one gh call fails partway through.","failureScenario":"A gh call fails (a handled condition) on the third of five medium IDs in a `defaults: true` call. One implementation records IDs 1–2 and fails; another records nothing. Either way the failure is reported, and a retry reuses the created issues through the marker search, so no wrong result is reported as success. The R5 single-ID test passes under both readings.","id":"F3","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"finding-id-scope-implicit","classStatement":"Whether review follow-ups are keyed by bare finding ID across scopes or within one scope depends on an unstated ID-uniqueness assumption.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1: \"one record per finding ID raised by the run's retained boundary `review` results ... The first raising result is the origin\" (design.md has the same wording). The spec doesn't say whether finding IDs are unique across review scopes (slice vs feature) and across paired members, or only within a scope's round chain.","failureScenario":"This only goes wrong if the existing #144 finding IDs are local to each result and not stable across a scope. In that case two unrelated findings, both named `F1`, from a slice review and a feature review merge into one record, and a `resolved` in one scope marks the other `fixed`. The approved design has the same wording, and ID stability is an existing-contract assumption the requirements can't confirm.","id":"F4","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"rubric-partial-ac-coverage","classStatement":"Some acceptance-criterion clauses have no rubric test, though every row stays binary.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"Several AC clauses have no rubric test. R4 tests goal and supervised autonomy but not mandated (allowed without instruction) or delegated (needs instruction). R8 checks only status and issue link, while AC8 also requires severity, authority kind and accepted lows. R6 doesn't test design.md's constraint that a run without the `followUps` marker still gets Completion enforcement.","failureScenario":"A wrong mandated/delegated authority check, a PR summary missing authority kind, or unmarked runs skipping Completion enforcement would pass R4, R8 or R6 as written. Each row is still binary, so this is a coverage suggestion.","id":"F5","severity":"low"}],"mandateAssessment":null,"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"FAIL: I can't authorize implementation yet. Two medium findings block it. Most of the spec follows the approved design closely and is testable. It covers derived review follow-ups, the non-review API, decisions and authority, issue filing, enforcement, the generated view, the PR surface, metrics, CLI and docs. Every rubric row R1–R11 maps to a plan step and an issue, and nothing goes beyond the design; the `followUpRedactions` setting is one way to meet the design's 'no client names' constraint. The first blocker is that AC1 doesn't say which repository a review follow-up belongs to when the finding's path is under no configured alias and isn't a requirements/ or planning/ path. That is ordinary reviewer output. The Failure model neither handles nor refuses it, and AC5 issue filing then has no defined target. The second blocker is that R3's pass condition asks for 'each of the six refusals'. AC3 lists five refusal categories, or seven distinct cases if each 'both or neither' is split, so the row can't be judged pass or fail. Its boundary cases (513 IDs, `followUps: []`, a `select` that matches nothing or an empty `select: {}`) are also left open. Three further low findings don't block: how a multi-ID new-work call behaves when one gh call fails, whether finding IDs could collide across review scopes, and AC clauses no rubric test covers. No mandate was supplied."}